1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,120
You order a jacket online.

2
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:03,970
It arrives on a Tuesday.

3
00:00:04,350 --> 00:00:07,540
By Thursday, you've decided
the sleeves are too short.

4
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:09,060
So you send it back.

5
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:10,540
The label is free.

6
00:00:10,540 --> 00:00:13,050
The refund shows up a few days later.

7
00:00:13,430 --> 00:00:15,330
For you, the story ends there.

8
00:00:15,330 --> 00:00:17,740
For the jacket, it's just getting started.

9
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:22,030
It will be scanned, trucked,
sorted, inspected, and judged.

10
00:00:22,030 --> 00:00:25,310
And every one of those steps
costs somebody something.

11
00:00:25,690 --> 00:00:28,010
So who actually pays for a free return?

12
00:00:28,910 --> 00:00:30,120
Start with the scale.

13
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:35,710
In October 2025, the National Retail
Federation and Happy Returns,

14
00:00:35,710 --> 00:00:41,870
a returns company owned by UPS,
published a report based on two surveys:

15
00:00:41,870 --> 00:00:45,903
one of shoppers, one of
large American retailers.

16
00:00:46,283 --> 00:00:49,883
Those retailers estimated that about 15.8

17
00:00:49,883 --> 00:00:53,583
percent of their sales
would come back in 2025.

18
00:00:53,963 --> 00:00:55,903
Across the whole industry,

19
00:00:55,903 --> 00:01:02,583
the report projected $849.9 billion
worth of returned merchandise.

20
00:01:02,963 --> 00:01:08,553
Online, the estimate was higher:
about 19.3 percent of online sales.

21
00:01:08,933 --> 00:01:11,163
Keep in mind what those numbers are.

22
00:01:11,163 --> 00:01:16,043
They measure the value of goods sent
back, not the cost of processing them.

23
00:01:16,043 --> 00:01:20,273
And they're projections for the
United States, not a final count.

